## ABSTRACT

This project focuses on the modeling and control of a Direct Current (DC) motor using MATLAB and Simulink. The project was divided into four tasks, including mathematical modeling of the DC motor, PID-based speed control, cascade position control, and integration of the complete control system. The objective was to analyze the dynamic behavior of the motor and improve its performance through appropriate control techniques. MATLAB and Simulink were used to design, simulate, and evaluate the control system. The project provided practical exposure to control system engineering and simulation-based analysis.

---

## 1. INTRODUCTION

DC motors are widely used in industrial automation, robotics, electric vehicles, conveyor systems, and servo mechanisms due to their simple construction and ease of control. Accurate speed and position control are essential for achieving efficient operation in such applications.

MATLAB and Simulink provide powerful tools for modeling dynamic systems and designing controllers. This project demonstrates the application of these tools for DC motor analysis and control.

## Objectives

- To understand the fundamentals of DC motors.
- To develop a mathematical model of a DC motor.
- To design a PID controller for speed regulation.
- To implement cascade position control.
- To integrate and evaluate the complete control system.

---

## 2. TASK 1 – DC MOTOR MATHEMATICAL MODELING

## Objective

To develop a mathematical model of the DC motor and analyze its dynamic behavior using MATLAB and Simulink.

## Theory

A DC motor converts electrical energy into mechanical energy. Its operation can be represented using electrical and mechanical equations. These equations are combined to obtain the transfer function of the motor, which describes the relationship between input voltage and output speed.

## Methodology

The motor parameters were selected based on standard DC motor characteristics. The transfer function was derived and implemented in MATLAB. A Simulink model was developed to represent the mathematical behavior of the motor and analyze its response.

## Results and Discussion

The developed model successfully represented the dynamic characteristics of the DC motor. The response showed stable behavior and demonstrated the relationship between input voltage and motor speed.

## Conclusion

The mathematical model accurately represented the DC motor dynamics and served as the foundation for subsequent controller design tasks.

---

## 3. TASK 2 – PID-BASED SPEED CONTROL

## Objective

To design and tune a PID controller for regulating the speed of the DC motor.

## Theory

PID control combines proportional, integral, and derivative actions to improve system performance. It reduces steady-state error, improves transient response, and enhances overall system stability.

## Methodology

A closed-loop control system was developed by connecting the PID controller with the DC motor model. Controller parameters were tuned to achieve a satisfactory speed response.

## Results and Discussion

The PID controller improved the speed regulation performance significantly. The controlled system exhibited faster response, reduced steady-state error, and improved stability compared to the uncontrolled system.

## Conclusion

The PID controller effectively regulated motor speed and improved system performance.

---

## 4. TASK 3 – CASCADE POSITION CONTROL

## Objective

To develop a cascade control structure for accurate motor position control.

## Theory

Cascade control utilizes two control loops. The outer loop controls motor position, while the inner loop regulates motor speed. This arrangement improves tracking accuracy and disturbance rejection.

## Methodology

A dual-loop control architecture was implemented using position and speed controllers. The position controller generated reference signals for the speed controller, which regulated motor operation.

## Results and Discussion

The cascade control system provided improved position tracking and reduced oscillatory behavior. The motor successfully followed the desired position reference with enhanced stability.

## Conclusion

The cascade control strategy achieved accurate and reliable position control of the DC motor.

---

## 5. TASK 4 – FINAL SYSTEM INTEGRATION

## Objective

To integrate all developed subsystems into a complete DC motor control system and evaluate overall performance.

## Theory

The integrated control system combines mathematical modeling, PID speed control, and cascade position control into a unified architecture. This enables simultaneous speed and position regulation.

## Methodology

The complete system was implemented using MATLAB and Simulink. The integrated model was analyzed to verify system stability and performance.

## Results and Discussion

The integrated system demonstrated stable operation, accurate position tracking, and effective speed regulation. The overall performance met the design objectives and control requirements.

## Conclusion

The complete DC motor control system was successfully implemented and validated through simulation.

